%[text] # DC Motors: From Dipole Torque to Continuous Rotation
%[text] [⇦ Main Menu](file:..\\MainMenu.m)
%[text] In [Magnetic Dipoles](file:.\\MagneticDipole.m), you saw that a current loop in a magnetic field feels a torque, a twist that tends to rotate the loop. That twist is useful, but it is not yet a motor. When the loop lines up with the field, the torque becomes zero. This script follows the extra ideas needed to keep a DC motor turning.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:8be2)
%%
%[text:tableOfContents]{"heading":"Table of Contents"}
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ma01] This live script is intended to be used with the code hidden. On the **View** tab of the MATLAB toolstrip, in the **View** section, select **Hide Code**. Alternately, select **Hide Code** using the icon ![live script code hidden icon](text:image:48b3) at the top right of the Live Editor pane.
%[text] ![Lightbulb mark](text:image:6849) Although the code is hidden, some interactivity requires familiarity with MATLAB. If you need more instruction, consider taking [MATLAB Onramp](https://matlabacademy.mathworks.com/details/matlab-onramp/gettingstarted), a free 2 hour online tutorial that teaches the essentials of MATLAB.
%[text] %[text:anchor:H_ma02] ![Warning symbol](text:image:33f4)   For an optimal experience, follow the instructions and steps in the given sequence. Proceed to a new section only after completing the preceding one. Some sections depend on variables created in prior sections and will generate errors if run out of order.
%[text] The ![Try this icon](text:image:38a9)  and  ![Exercise icon](text:image:705e) icons refer to two different types of interactive activities that you will find in this script. The ![Try this icon](text:image:1f2a)  usually indicates an interaction where you will explore the visualization of some concept introduced in this script. The ![Exercise icon](text:image:0a81) interactions are designed to challenge your understanding of those concepts and may be used for grading and completion checks by your professor.
%%
%[text] ## Recap: From Dipole Moment to Motor Torque
%[text] In [Magnetic Dipoles](file:.\\MagneticDipole.m), you learned that the magnetic dipole moment of a current loop is $\\vec{m} = IA\\hat{n}$. A dipole in an external field $\\vec{B}$ has torque $\\tau = mB\\sin(\\theta)$, where $\\theta$ is the angle between $\\vec{m}$ and $\\vec{B}$, and $B =  \\|\\vec{B}\\|$. For a motor coil with $N$ turns of wire, the dipole moment is $N$ times larger, giving the motor torque equation:
%[text]{"align":"center"} %[text:anchor:M_931c] $ \\tau = NIAB\\sin(\\theta) \\qquad (1) $
%[text] Where:
%[text] - $N$ is the **number of turns** in the coil,
%[text] - $I$ is the **current** (in **A**),
%[text] - $A$ is the **coil area** (in **m²**),
%[text] - $B$ is the **magnetic flux density** (in **T**),
%[text] - $\\theta$ is the **angle** between the coil's dipole moment and the magnetic field. \
%[text] [This equation](internal:M_931c) is the starting point. Here, the sign of $\\tau$ tells you which way the chosen rotation axis points; the absolute value $|\\tau|$ tells you how strong the twist is. First, change the parameters and watch which ones make the twist stronger.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:60ab) **Try.** Before you run the section, predict which parameter will change the peak torque most. Then adjust $N$, $I$, $B$, and coil radius to test your prediction.
N = 1; % Number of turns %[control:spinner:40b2]{"position":[5,6]}
I = 2; % Current (A) %[control:slider:3b07]{"position":[5,6]}
B = 2; % Magnetic field (T) %[control:slider:9043]{"position":[5,6]}
CoilRadius = 0.05; % Coil radius (m) %[control:slider:2c0b]{"position":[14,18]}
  %[control:button:ma_run1]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeTorqueParameters(N, I, B, CoilRadius, false);

function VisualizeTorqueParameters(N, I, B, CoilRadius, ShowZeroTorqueLabels)
if nargin < 5
    ShowZeroTorqueLabels = false;
end

% Convert coil radius to area, then evaluate torque over one revolution.
A = pi * CoilRadius^2;
theta = linspace(0, 2*pi, 200);
tauMilliNewtonMeters = N * I * A * B * sin(theta) * 1000;

figure(Position=[100 100 600 400])
plot(rad2deg(theta), tauMilliNewtonMeters, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1)
xlabel("\theta (degrees)")
ylabel("\tau (mN\cdot m)")
title("Torque vs. Angle")
xline([0 180 360], "--", Alpha=0.3)
yline(0, "-", Alpha=0.3)
ylim(computePaddedLimits(tauMilliNewtonMeters))
grid on

if ShowZeroTorqueLabels
    zeroTorqueAngles = [0 180 360];
    zeroTorqueValues = zeros(size(zeroTorqueAngles));
    hold on
    plot(zeroTorqueAngles, zeroTorqueValues, "o", ...
        MarkerSize=8, LineWidth=1.5, MarkerFaceColor="white", SeriesIndex=2)

    labelHeight = 0.12 * max(abs(ylim));
    for zeroTorqueAngle = zeroTorqueAngles
        text(zeroTorqueAngle, labelHeight, "\tau = 0", ...
            HorizontalAlignment="center", VerticalAlignment="bottom", FontWeight="bold")
    end
    hold off
end
end
%%
%[text] ![Reflect icon](text:image:8246) **Reflect.** The torque is zero at $\\theta = 0\\degree $ and $\\theta = 180\\degree $, the aligned and anti-aligned positions. Between these points, the torque turns the loop toward alignment. Once aligned, there is no twist left to keep the loop moving in the same direction.
%%
%[text] Click Show Zero Torque Points to rerun the plot with the zero-torque angles labeled.
ShowZeroTorqueLabels = true;
  %[control:button:449a]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeTorqueParameters(N, I, B, CoilRadius, ShowZeroTorqueLabels);
%%
%[text] ## The Problem: Oscillation Is Not Rotation
%[text] The torque equation $\\tau = NIAB\\sin(\\theta)$ also shows why a single current loop does not keep spinning. Here $\\theta$ is the signed angular displacement from the aligned position. The **restoring torque** points back toward that position, so $\\tau\_{\\text{restore}} = -NIAB\\sin(\\theta)$. If the loop moves past alignment, the torque reverses and pulls it back.
%[text] That back-and-forth motion is oscillation. Friction and air resistance make the oscillation damped, meaning each swing is smaller than the last until the loop stops at equilibrium.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:13fe) **Try.** Release the loop from an initial angle. Before running, predict whether more damping will make it stop faster or overshoot more. This uses the same $N$, $I$, $B$, and coil radius you set above. The damping coefficient, in N·m·s/rad, represents friction and air resistance.
Damping = 0.001; % Damping coefficient %[control:editfield:2f35]{"position":[11,16]}
InitialAngle = 90; % Initial angle (degrees) %[control:slider:1111]{"position":[16,18]}
  %[control:button:ma_run2]{"position":[1,2]}
SimulateLoopDynamics(N, I, B, pi*CoilRadius^2, Damping, InitialAngle);

function SimulateLoopDynamics(N, I, B, A, Damping, InitialAngle)
tauMax = N * I * A * B;

% Choose a moment of inertia that keeps the motion visible on a short time scale.
J = tauMax / 9;
b = Damping;

% State y contains angular position and angular speed.
odefun = @(t, y) [y(2); -(tauMax/J)*sin(y(1)) - (b/J)*y(2)];
y0 = [deg2rad(InitialAngle); 0];
tspan = [0 10];
[t, y] = ode45(odefun, tspan, y0);

thetaDeg = rad2deg(y(:,1));
torque = -tauMax * sin(y(:,1)) * 1000;

figure(Position=[100 100 800 500])
tiledlayout(2,1,TileSpacing="compact")

nexttile
plot(t, thetaDeg, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1)
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel("\theta (degrees)")
title("Angular Position vs. Time")
yline(0, "--", "Aligned", Alpha=0.3)
grid on

nexttile
plot(t, torque, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=2)
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel("\tau (mN\cdot m)")
title("Torque vs. Time")
yline(0, "--", Alpha=0.3)
grid on
end
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:9b20) **Exercise 1.** As the current loop rotates past the aligned position ($\\theta = 0\\degree $) with constant current direction, what happens to the torque?
CheckExercise1("Select") %[control:dropdown:9dcc]{"position":[16,24]}

function CheckExercise1(Answer)
switch Answer
    case "Select"
        warning("Please select an answer.")
    case "Remains zero"
        warning("Incorrect. The torque is zero only at the exact alignment point. Just past alignment, sin(θ) changes sign.")
    case "Reverses direction"
        displayFormula('"' + "Correct! As the loop passes through alignment, sin(θ) changes sign," + '"')
        displayFormula('"' + "reversing the torque. This is why the loop oscillates rather than rotates continuously." + '"')
    case "Doubles in magnitude"
        warning("Incorrect. The torque magnitude depends on sin(θ), not on passing through alignment.")
    case "Becomes maximum"
        warning("Incorrect. Maximum torque occurs at θ = 90°, not at alignment.")
    otherwise
        warning("Please select a valid answer.")
end
end
%%
%[text] ## The Commutator: Reversing Current at the Right Moment
%[text] A brushed DC motor uses a commutator, a rotating mechanical switch, to keep the torque from changing direction. A split-ring commutator has two conducting half-rings attached to the coil. Stationary brushes press against the half-rings and connect the coil to the power supply.
%[text] As the coil turns, each brush changes from one half-ring to the other every half turn. That swap reverses the coil current near $\\theta = 0\\degree $ and $\\theta = 180\\degree $, just when an uncommutated coil would start producing torque in the wrong direction.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![Split-ring commutator diagram showing brushes, split ring, coil, magnetic field, and current reversal](text:image:1f28)
%[text] By flipping the current at the moment the torque would reverse, the commutator converts the sinusoidal torque into a **rectified** waveform. Rectified means the negative part has been flipped upward:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\tau\_{\\text{commutated}} = NIAB|\\sin(\\theta)| \\qquad (2) $
%[text] The torque is now always positive or zero, so the loop is pushed in the same rotational direction. If the coil speed is roughly uniform, the angle-averaged torque over one half-cycle is:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\tau\_{\\text{avg}} = \\frac{2}{\\pi} NIAB \\qquad (3) $
%[text] To see what this does to motion, compare the uncommutated and commutated cases with the rotational form of Newton's second law. The state variables are angular position $\\theta$ and angular speed $\\omega$:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $J\\frac{d^2\\theta}{dt^2}=\\tau\_{\\text{motor}}+\\tau\_{\\text{damping}}, \\\\ \\qquad \\tau\_{\\text{damping}}=-b\\frac{d\\theta}{dt}$
%[text] Without a commutator, $\\tau\_{\\text{motor}}=NIAB\\sin(\\theta)$. With a commutator, $\\tau\_{\\text{motor}}=NIAB|\\sin(\\theta)|$.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:1692) **Try.** Click **Run** and compare each row from left to right. Without the commutator, the loop oscillates and settles near alignment. With the commutator, torque keeps pushing in the same direction until its average value balances losses. The bottom row shows motor torque only.
  %[control:button:ma_run3]{"position":[1,2]}

VisualizeCommutatorEffect(1,... %[control:spinner:75ea]{"position":[27,28]}
    2,... %[control:slider:8fcb]{"position":[5,6]}
    2,... %[control:slider:6d2c]{"position":[5,6]}
    pi*0.05^2,... %[control:slider:640a]{"position":[8,12]}
    0.01,... %[control:editfield:32ee]{"position":[5,9]}
    0, ... %[control:slider:2db6]{"position":[5,6]}
    false); %[control:checkbox:53ec]{"position":[5,10]}

function VisualizeCommutatorEffect(N, I, B, A, Damping, InitialAngle,SwitchUnit)
tauMax = N * I * A * B;
J = max(tauMax/9, 1e-6);
b = max(Damping, 1e-6);
terminalSpeed = (2/pi) * tauMax / b;
simulationTime = min(max(8*J/b, 10), 30);
tspan = [0 simulationTime];
y0 = [deg2rad(InitialAngle); 0];

% Compare constant current with current that reverses every half turn.
noCommutatorOde = @(~, y) [y(2); (-tauMax*sin(y(1)) - b*y(2)) / J];
commutatorOde = @(~, y) [y(2); (tauMax*abs(sin(y(1))) - b*y(2)) / J];
[tNoComm, yNoComm] = ode45(noCommutatorOde, tspan, y0);
[tComm, yComm] = ode45(commutatorOde, tspan, y0);

motorTorqueNoComm = -tauMax * sin(yNoComm(:,1));
motorTorqueComm = tauMax * abs(sin(yComm(:,1)));

figure(Position=[100 100 750 450])
tl = tiledlayout(2,3,TileSpacing="compact");
odeStr = "$$J\frac{d^{2}\theta}{dt^{2}}=\tau_{\mathrm{motor}}-b\frac{d\theta}{dt}$$";
title(tl, odeStr, Interpreter="latex", FontSize=14);

% ---- Plot Position
if SwitchUnit
    Label = "\theta (degrees)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) rad2deg(x);
else
    Label = "\theta (rad)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) x;
end

ax1 = nexttile(1);
plot(tNoComm, ScaleFun(yNoComm(:,1)), LineWidth=2, DisplayName="Without commutator", SeriesIndex=1)
grid on
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
title("Without commutator")

ax4 = nexttile(4);
plot(tComm, ScaleFun(yComm(:,1)), LineWidth=2, DisplayName="With commutator", SeriesIndex=2)
title("With commutator")
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
grid on

% ---- Plot Speed
if SwitchUnit
    Label = "\omega (rpm)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) x*60/(2*pi);
else
    Label = "\omega (rad/s)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) x;
end

ax2 = nexttile(2);
plot(tNoComm, ScaleFun(yNoComm(:,2)), LineWidth=2, DisplayName="Without commutator", SeriesIndex=1)
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
grid on

ax5 = nexttile(5);
plot(tComm, ScaleFun(yComm(:,2)), LineWidth=2, DisplayName="With commutator", SeriesIndex=2)
yline(terminalSpeed, "--", "\omega_{steady}", Alpha=1, LabelVerticalAlignment="top",LabelHorizontalAlignment="left")
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
grid on

% ---- Plot Torque
if SwitchUnit
    Label = "\tau (N\cdot m)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) x;
else
    Label = "\tau (mN\cdot m)";
    ScaleFun = @(x) 1000*x;
end

ax3 = nexttile(3);
plot(tNoComm, ScaleFun(motorTorqueNoComm), SeriesIndex=1, LineWidth=2, DisplayName="Motor")
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
grid on

ax6 = nexttile(6);
plot(tComm, ScaleFun(motorTorqueComm), LineWidth=2, DisplayName="Motor", SeriesIndex=2)
xlabel("Time (s)")
ylabel(Label)
grid on

% ---- Link the axes
linkaxes([ax1 ax4])
linkaxes([ax2 ax5])
linkaxes([ax3 ax6])

end
%%
%[text] ![Reflect icon](text:image:7892) **Reflect.** The commutator changes the sign of the current when the torque would reverse. In this damping-only model, the motor reaches a steady speed when the average commutated torque balances losses.
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:6b59) **Exercise 2.** A motor has $N = 50$ turns, carries a current of $I = 3$ A, has a circular coil of radius $r = 4$ cm, and operates in a field of $B = 0.8$ T. Calculate the average torque (in N·m) with commutation. Round your answer to four decimal places.
CheckExercise2("") %[control:editfield:1c13]{"position":[16,18]}
  %[control:button:1418]{"position":[1,2]}

function CheckExercise2(Answer)
arguments
    Answer = ""
end

turns = 50;
current = 3;
radius = 0.04;
field = 0.8;
area = pi * radius^2;
CorrectAnswer = (2/pi) * turns * current * area * field;
Tolerance = 5e-5;

% Accept Answer as a string expression; evaluate to numeric.
if Answer == ""
    displayFormula('"' + "Please enter your answer as a string expression in N·m (e.g. '0.0123' or '2/pi*50*3')." + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: Use τ_avg = (2/π) × N I A B." + '"')
    return
end

% If numeric provided, convert to string for evaluation consistency
if isnumeric(Answer)
    AnswerStr = num2str(Answer);
else
    AnswerStr = string(Answer);
end

% Try to evaluate the string safely
try
    % Evaluate in function workspace
    EvalAnswer = eval(AnswerStr);
catch
    warning("Could not evaluate the provided string. Ensure it is a valid MATLAB expression.")
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: Use τ_avg = (2/π) × N I A B and A = π × r²." + '"')
    return
end

if isempty(EvalAnswer) || ~isfinite(EvalAnswer)
    warning("The evaluated answer is not a finite number.")
    return
end

if abs(EvalAnswer - CorrectAnswer) < Tolerance
    displayFormula('"' + "Correct! The average torque is " + string(round(CorrectAnswer, 4)) + " N·m." + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "Recall: τ_avg = (2/π) × NIAB" + '"')
else
    warning("Incorrect. Use τ_avg = (2/π) × NIAB with the given values.")
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: A = π × r² = π × (0.04)² = " + string(round(area, 6)) + " m²" + '"')
end
end
%%
%[text] ## Smoothing the Ride: Multiple Coils and Pole Pairs
%[text] A single commutated coil produces a torque waveform with large ripple, meaning the torque repeatedly rises and falls during each revolution instead of staying steady. In this script, ripple is reported as a percentage using the largest and smallest torque values in one cycle:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\text{ripple} = 100\\% \\times \\frac{\\tau\_{\\max}-\\tau\_{\\min}}{\\tau\_{\\max}+\\tau\_{\\min}} $
%[text] If the waveform drops all the way to zero, this metric gives 100% ripple. That uneven torque can cause vibration. Two design choices help smooth the motion.
%[text] Multiple coils\*\*:\*\* By placing $c$ coils around the armature, each offset by $180\\degree  / c$, the torque contributions overlap:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\theta\_k = (k-1) \\times \\frac{180\\degree }{c}, \\quad k = 1, 2, \\ldots, c \\qquad (4) $
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\tau\_{\\text{total}}(\\theta) = NIAB \\sum\_{k=1}^{c} |\\sin(\\theta + \\theta\_k)| \\qquad (5) $
%[text] For example, with 3 coils the offsets are $0\\degree $, $60\\degree $, and $120\\degree $. Each coil reaches peak torque at a different angle, so the total torque never drops to zero.
%[text] Pole pairs\*\*:\*\* A motor with $p$ pole pairs creates $p$ complete electrical cycles per mechanical revolution. More pole pairs make the torque pulses arrive more often. In this simplified model, ripple size depends mainly on how many coil waveforms overlap.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:4565) **Try.** Adjust the number of coils and click **Run**. Watch the individual coil waveforms first, then the total. The question is whether overlapping coils keep the total torque from dropping too low.
NumCoils = 1; % Number of coils %[control:spinner:4204]{"position":[12,13]}
  %[control:button:ma_run4]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeMultipleCoils(NumCoils, N, I, B, pi*CoilRadius^2);

function VisualizeMultipleCoils(NumCoils, N, I, B, A)
c = max(1, round(NumCoils));
theta = linspace(0, 2*pi, 500);
tauMax = N * I * A * B;
offsets = (0:c-1) * pi / c;

figure(Position=[100 100 800 500])
tiledlayout(2,1,TileSpacing="compact")

nexttile
hold on
tauTotal = zeros(size(theta));
for k = 1:c
    tauK = tauMax * abs(sin(theta + offsets(k)));
    tauTotal = tauTotal + tauK;
    if c <= 8
        h = plot(rad2deg(theta), tauK*1000, "--", DisplayName="Coil " + string(k), ...
            SeriesIndex=mod(k, 7) + 1);
        h.Color(4) = 0.4;
    end
end
plot(rad2deg(theta), tauTotal*1000, Color=[0 0 0], LineWidth=2.2, DisplayName="Total")
xlabel("\theta (degrees)")
ylabel("\tau (mN\cdot m)")
title("Torque Waveform: " + string(c) + " Coil(s)")
legend(Location="best")
grid on
hold off

nexttile
maxC = max(c, 6);
ripples = zeros(1, maxC);
for numC = 1:maxC
    offs = (0:numC-1) * pi / numC;
    total = zeros(size(theta));
    for j = 1:numC
        total = total + abs(sin(theta + offs(j)));
    end
    ripples(numC) = (max(total) - min(total)) / (max(total) + min(total)) * 100;
end
stem(1:maxC, ripples, LineWidth=1.5, MarkerSize=6)
hold on
plot(c, ripples(c), "o", MarkerSize=12, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=2)
hold off
xlabel("Number of Coils")
ylabel("Ripple (%)")
title("Torque Ripple: " + string(round(ripples(c), 1)) + "% for " + string(c) + " coil(s)")
grid on
end
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:01cf) **Try.** Change the number of pole pairs and compare the spacing between torque pulses. Pole pairs make the pulses arrive more often; coils make the total torque smoother.
NumCoils = 1; % Number of coils %[control:spinner:01d8]{"position":[12,13]}
PolePairs = 1; % Number of pole pairs %[control:spinner:550b]{"position":[13,14]}
  %[control:button:ma_run5]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizePoleEffect(PolePairs, NumCoils, N, I, B, pi*CoilRadius^2);

function VisualizePoleEffect(PolePairs, NumCoils, N, I, B, A)
p = max(1, round(PolePairs));
c = max(1, round(NumCoils));
thetaMech = linspace(0, 2*pi, 500);
thetaElec = p * thetaMech;
tauMax = N * I * A * B;
offsets = (0:c-1) * pi / c;

figure(Position=[100 100 1000 420])
tiledlayout(1,2,TileSpacing="compact")

nexttile
tauTotal = zeros(size(thetaMech));
for k = 1:c
    tauTotal = tauTotal + tauMax * abs(sin(thetaElec + offsets(k)));
end
plot(rad2deg(thetaMech), tauTotal*1000, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1)
ylim([0 max(tauTotal*1200)])
xlabel("Mechanical Angle (degrees)")
ylabel("\tau (mN\cdot m)")
title(string(c) + " Coil(s), " + string(p) + " Pole Pair(s): " + string(2*c*p) + " Torque Pulses / Revolution")
yline(mean(tauTotal)*1000, "--", {"\tau_{avg} = " + string(round(mean(tauTotal)*1000, 2)) + " mN\cdot m   ",""} , Alpha=1)
grid on

nexttile
drawMotorCrossSection(c, p, 2*c*p)
title("Motor Schematic", FontSize=10)
end
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:63b7) **Exercise 3.** A motor has 3 coils and 2 pole pairs. How many torque pulses does the motor produce per mechanical revolution?
CheckExercise3("Select") %[control:dropdown:ma_ex3]{"position":[16,24]}

function CheckExercise3(Answer)
switch Answer
    case "Select"
        warning("Please select an answer.")
    case "6"
        warning("Incorrect. Remember that each coil produces 2 pulses per electrical cycle, and there are p electrical cycles per mechanical revolution (with p being the number of pole pairs).")
    case "8"
        warning("Incorrect. The formula is 2 × c × p, where c is the number of coils and p is the number of pole pairs.")
    case "12"
        displayFormula('"' + "Correct! With 3 coils and 2 pole pairs, the motor produces 2 × 3 × 2 = 12 torque pulses per revolution." + '"')
    case "24"
        warning("Incorrect. Each coil produces 2 (not 4) pulses per electrical cycle.")
    otherwise
        warning("Please select a valid answer.")
end
end
%%
%[text] ![Reflect icon](text:image:6406) **Reflect.** Adding more coils smooths the torque, and adding pole pairs makes the torque pulses arrive more frequently. Both choices have engineering trade-offs. More coils means more copper, more weight, and higher manufacturing cost. More pole pairs requires more magnets and a more complex design. Many small brushed DC motors use a small number of coils and pole pairs, balancing smoothness against practical constraints.
%%
%[text] ## Back-EMF and the Speed-Torque Relationship
%[text] A spinning DC motor also behaves like a generator. As the coil turns through the magnetic field, it produces a voltage that opposes the supply voltage. This opposing voltage is called back-EMF, short for back electromotive force.
%[text] At standstill, there is no back-EMF, so the supply drives a large current through the winding resistance. This condition is called stall: the motor is powered, but the shaft is not turning. For example, a power drill can stall when the bit jams in hard material. The motor then draws its largest current, produces its largest starting torque, and can heat quickly if it stays stalled. As speed increases, back-EMF grows, current falls, and the motor can provide less torque.
%[text]{"align":"center"} ![](text:image:6bb0)
%[text] For the rest of the script, we use an ideal effective motor constant. This replaces the pulsing commutated torque with an average torque-per-current constant, so you can connect coil geometry to speed-torque curves without simulating every rotor angle. For one commutated coil, $K\_{\\text{eff}}=(2/\\pi)NAB$. For $c$ overlapping coils in the later design model, $K\_{\\text{eff}}=(2/\\pi)cNAB$. In SI units, the ideal torque and back-EMF constants are equal: $K\_t=K\_e=K\_{\\text{eff}}$.
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ E\_{\\text{back}} = K\_e \\omega, \\qquad I = \\frac{V-K\_e\\omega}{R}, \\qquad \\tau = K\_t I \\qquad (6) $
%[text] The result is a straight-line speed-torque relationship. At no load, the ideal motor reaches $\\omega\_0=V/K\_e$. At stall, where $\\omega=0$, it produces $\\tau\_{\\text{stall}}=K\_tV/R$.
%[text] Power is the rate of energy transfer. For rotation, a torque $\\tau$ acting while the shaft turns at angular speed $\\omega$ transfers mechanical power $P\_{\\text{mech}} = \\tau\\omega$. For a circuit, voltage is energy per unit charge and current is charge per second, so their product gives electrical input power: $P\_{\\text{in}} = VI$.
%[text] Efficiency tells us what fraction of that electrical input power becomes useful mechanical output power:
%[text]{"align":"center"} $ \\eta = \\frac{P\_{\\text{mech}}}{P\_{\\text{in}}} = \\frac{\\tau\\omega}{VI} \\times 100\\% $
%[text] In this ideal model, efficiency is 0% at stall because the shaft is not moving, so $P\_{\\text{mech}}=0$ even though current is large. The curve can approach 100% only near no load, where current and torque approach zero, so almost no useful power is delivered. Real motors never reach 100% because winding resistance, friction, magnetic losses, brush losses, and heating always consume some input power.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:7208) **Try.** Set the supply voltage and winding resistance, then click **Run**. Look for three linked changes: current falls as speed rises, torque falls with current, and mechanical power peaks between stall and no load. The efficiency plot is a best-case limit for this ideal model.
V = 12; % Supply voltage (V) %[control:editfield:ma_volt]{"position":[5,7]}
R = 2; % Winding resistance (Ω) %[control:editfield:ma_resist]{"position":[5,6]}

% Ensure required variables exist; if not, initialize with standard values
if ~exist('N', 'var') || isempty(N)
    N = 100; % default turns
end
if ~exist('CoilRadius', 'var') || isempty(CoilRadius)
    CoilRadius = 0.025; % default radius in meters (2.5 cm)
end
if ~exist('B', 'var') || isempty(B)
    B = 0.6; % default magnetic field in tesla
end

  %[control:button:631d]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeMotorCharacteristics(V, R, N, pi*CoilRadius^2, B);

function VisualizeMotorCharacteristics(V, R, N, A, B)
Ke = computeEffectiveMotorConstant(N, A, B);
Kt = Ke;
omega0 = V / Ke;
tauStall = Kt * V / R;

% Sweep from stall to no load to build the ideal motor curves.
omega = linspace(0, omega0, 200);
tau = tauStall * (1 - omega/omega0);
current = (V - Ke*omega) / R;
Pmech = tau .* omega;
Pelec = V * current;
efficiency = NaN(size(omega));
validEfficiency = Pelec > 0;
efficiency(validEfficiency) = Pmech(validEfficiency) ./ Pelec(validEfficiency) * 100;

figure(Position=[100 100 900 600])
tiledlayout(2,2,TileSpacing="compact")

nexttile
plot(omega, tau, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1)
ylabel("\tau (N\cdot m)")
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
title("Speed-Torque Curve")
yline(tauStall, "--", "\tau_{stall}", Alpha=0.3)
xline(omega0, "--", "\omega_0", Alpha=0.3)
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, current, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=2)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("I (A)")
title("Current vs. Speed")
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, Pmech, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=3)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("P (W)")
title("Mechanical Power vs. Speed")
[Pmax, idx] = max(Pmech);
xline(omega(idx), "--", "P_{max} = " + string(round(Pmax, 2)) + " W", Alpha=0.3)
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, efficiency, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=4)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("\eta (%)")
title("Efficiency vs. Speed")
ylim([0 100])
grid on
end
%%
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:5b74) **Exercise 4.** A motor has $N = 100$ turns, coil area $A = 20$ $\\text{cm}^2$, operates in a field of $B = 0.6$ T, is supplied with $V = 24$ V, and has winding resistance $R = 4$ Ω. Calculate the no-load speed $\\omega\_0$ (in rad/s) and the stall torque $\\tau\_{\\text{stall}}$ (in N·m). Round speed to the nearest rad/s and torque to two decimal places.
CheckExercise4(0,... %[control:editfield:ma_speed]{"position":[16,17]}
    0) %[control:editfield:107e]{"position":[5,6]}

function CheckExercise4(SpeedAnswer, TorqueAnswer)
turns = 100;
area = 20e-4;
field = 0.6;
voltage = 24;
resistance = 4;

Ke = computeEffectiveMotorConstant(turns, area, field);
Kt = Ke;
CorrectSpeed = voltage / Ke;
CorrectTorque = Kt * voltage / resistance;
SpeedTol = CorrectSpeed * 0.05;
TorqueTol = CorrectTorque * 0.05;

if SpeedAnswer == 0 && TorqueAnswer == 0
    warning("Please enter your answers for ω₀ and τ_stall.")
elseif abs(SpeedAnswer - CorrectSpeed) < SpeedTol && abs(TorqueAnswer - CorrectTorque) < TorqueTol
    displayFormula('"' + "Correct! ω₀ = V/Kₑ = " + string(round(CorrectSpeed, 1)) + " rad/s and τ_stall = KₜV/R = " + string(round(CorrectTorque, 2)) + " N·m" + '"')
elseif abs(SpeedAnswer - CorrectSpeed) < SpeedTol
    displayFormula('"' + "Speed is correct! But check your stall torque calculation." + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: τ_stall = Kₜ × V / R, where Kₜ = (2/π)NAB = " + string(round(Kt, 3)) + '"')
elseif abs(TorqueAnswer - CorrectTorque) < TorqueTol
    displayFormula('"' + "Stall torque is correct! But check your no-load speed calculation." + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: ω₀ = V / Kₑ, where Kₑ = (2/π)NAB = " + string(round(Ke, 3)) + '"')
else
    warning("Both answers are incorrect. Start by computing Kₑ = Kₜ = (2/π)NAB.")
    displayFormula('"' + "Hint: Kₑ = (2/π) × " + string(turns) + " × " + string(round(area, 4)) + " × " + string(round(field, 1)) + " = " + string(round(Ke, 4)) + '"')
end
end
%%
%[text] ![Reflect icon](text:image:3fdb) **Reflect.** Why does a power drill slow down when you press harder on a screw? A higher load requires more torque. More torque requires more current, which increases the voltage drop across the winding resistance. Less voltage remains to overcome the back-EMF, so the motor slows down. The ideal trend is simple: higher load, lower speed.
%%
%[text] ## Design Your Motor
%[text] You now have the key pieces for a simplified DC motor: coil torque, commutation, multi-coil smoothing, pole pairs, and the back-EMF speed-torque relationship. In this section, you will combine these ideas in a motor design workspace.
%%
%[text] ![Try this icon](text:image:9295) **Try.** Adjust the motor parameters below and click **Run**. Find one change that raises stall torque, one that raises no-load speed, and one that reduces ripple. Then identify the trade-off each change creates.
DesignTurns = 10; % Number of turns %[control:spinner:49b6]{"position":[15,17]}
DesignField = 0.5; % Magnetic field (T) %[control:editfield:7e80]{"position":[15,18]}
DesignCoilRadius = 0.05; % Coil radius (m) %[control:editfield:21b7]{"position":[20,24]}
NumCoils = 2; % Number of coils %[control:spinner:8f01]{"position":[12,13]}
PolePairs = 1; % Number of pole pairs %[control:spinner:171a]{"position":[13,14]}
DesignVoltage = 12; % Supply voltage (V) %[control:editfield:462f]{"position":[17,19]}
DesignResistance = 1; % Winding resistance (Ω) %[control:editfield:1dde]{"position":[20,21]}

  %[control:button:ma_run7]{"position":[1,2]}
VisualizeMotorDesign(NumCoils, DesignTurns, DesignCoilRadius, DesignField, PolePairs, DesignVoltage, DesignResistance);

function VisualizeMotorDesign(NumCoils, N, CoilRadius, B, PolePairs, V, R)
Design = struct( ...
    "NumCoils", NumCoils, ...
    "Turns", N, ...
    "CoilRadius", CoilRadius, ...
    "Field", B, ...
    "PolePairs", PolePairs, ...
    "Voltage", V, ...
    "Resistance", R);
if ~isValidDesign(Design)
    warning("Use practical positive values: 1-12 coils, 1-500 turns, radius 0.005-0.20 m, B 0.01-2 T, 1-8 pole pairs, V > 0, and R > 0.")
    return
end

% Combine the earlier ideas into one dashboard: torque ripple, speed, power, and current.
A = pi * CoilRadius^2;
c = max(1, round(NumCoils));
p = max(1, round(PolePairs));
Ke = computeEffectiveMotorConstant(N, A, B, c);
Kt = Ke;
omega0 = V / Ke;
tauStall = Kt * V / R;
pulseCount = 2 * c * p;

% Build a normalized torque waveform so ripple can be compared across designs.
thetaMech = linspace(0, 2*pi, 500);
thetaElec = p * thetaMech;
offsets = (0:c-1) * pi / c;
normalizedShape = zeros(size(thetaMech));
for k = 1:c
    normalizedShape = normalizedShape + abs(sin(thetaElec + offsets(k)));
end
normalizedShape = normalizedShape * pi / (2*c);
tauWave = tauStall * normalizedShape;
ripple = (max(tauWave) - min(tauWave)) / (max(tauWave) + min(tauWave)) * 100;

% Compute ideal speed-torque, power, current, and efficiency curves.
omega = linspace(0, omega0, 200);
tauLine = tauStall * (1 - omega/omega0);
Pmech = tauLine .* omega;
current = (V - Ke*omega) / R;
Pelec = V * current;
efficiency = NaN(size(omega));
validEfficiency = Pelec > 0;
efficiency(validEfficiency) = Pmech(validEfficiency) ./ Pelec(validEfficiency) * 100;

figure(Position=[100 100 1100 1100])
tiledlayout(3,3,TileSpacing="compact")
% Show key design objectives as a list
nexttile([1 3])
objectiveList = { ...
    "K_{eff} = " + string(round(Ke, 4)) + " N·m/A", ...
    "\omega_0 = " + string(round(omega0)) + " rad/s", ...
    "\tau_{stall} = " + string(round(tauStall, 3)) + " N·m", ...
    "pulses/rev = " + string(pulseCount)};

% Split into two columns
n = numel(objectiveList);
col = ceil(n/2);
leftList = objectiveList(1:col);
rightList = objectiveList(col+1:end);
text(-0.05, 0.5, "Design Objectives:", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=12, HorizontalAlignment="left")

% Left column
for i = 1:numel(leftList)
    text(-0.05, 0.5 - 0.15*i, "- " + leftList{i}, FontSize=11, HorizontalAlignment="left")
end

% Right column (if any)
for i = 1:numel(rightList)
    text(0.35, 0.5 - 0.15*i, "- " + rightList{i}, FontSize=11, HorizontalAlignment="left")
end
axis off

nexttile
plot(rad2deg(thetaMech), tauWave*1000, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=1)
xlabel("Mechanical Angle (degrees)")
ylabel("\tau (mN\cdot m)")
title("Torque Waveform (Ripple: " + string(round(ripple, 1)) + "%)")
grid on

nexttile
plot(tauLine, omega, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=2)
xlabel("\tau (N\cdot m)")
ylabel("\omega (rad/s)")
title("Speed-Torque")
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, Pmech, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=3)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("P (W)")
title("Mechanical Power")
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, efficiency, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=4)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("\eta (%)")
title("Efficiency")
ylim([0 100])
grid on

nexttile
plot(omega, current, LineWidth=2, SeriesIndex=5)
xlabel("\omega (rad/s)")
ylabel("I (A)")
title("Current vs. Speed")
grid on

nexttile
drawMotorCrossSection(c, p, pulseCount)
title(string(c) + " coils, " + string(p) + " pole pairs, " + string(pulseCount) + " pulses/rev", FontSize=9)
end

function drawMotorCrossSection(c, p, pulseCount)
hold on
theta = linspace(0, 2*pi, 200);
statorRadius = 1.0;
rotorRadius = 0.55;
shaftRadius = 0.12;

plot(statorRadius*cos(theta), statorRadius*sin(theta), "k-", LineWidth=2)
plot(1.1*cos(theta), 1.1*sin(theta), "k-", LineWidth=1)

numMagnets = 2*p;
magnetArc = 0.7*(2*pi/numMagnets);
for magnetIndex = 1:numMagnets
    centerAngle = (magnetIndex - 1)*2*pi/numMagnets;
    arcAngles = linspace(centerAngle - magnetArc/2, centerAngle + magnetArc/2, 30);
    xInner = 0.78*cos(arcAngles);
    yInner = 0.78*sin(arcAngles);
    xOuter = 0.98*cos(fliplr(arcAngles));
    yOuter = 0.98*sin(fliplr(arcAngles));

    if mod(magnetIndex, 2) == 1
        poleColor = [0.85 0.3 0.3];
        poleLabel = "N";
    else
        poleColor = [0.3 0.3 0.85];
        poleLabel = "S";
    end

    fill([xInner xOuter], [yInner yOuter], poleColor, FaceAlpha=0.55, EdgeColor="k")
    text(0.88*cos(centerAngle), 0.88*sin(centerAngle), poleLabel, ...
        HorizontalAlignment="center", FontWeight="bold", Color="w")
end

plot(rotorRadius*cos(theta), rotorRadius*sin(theta), "k-", LineWidth=1.5)
fill(shaftRadius*cos(theta), shaftRadius*sin(theta), [0.7 0.7 0.7], EdgeColor="k")

slotArc = 0.5*(2*pi/(2*c));
coilColors = colororder;
for coilIndex = 1:c
    coilOffset = (coilIndex - 1)*pi/c;
    for sideAngle = [0 pi]
        slotCenter = coilOffset + sideAngle;
        slotTheta = linspace(slotCenter - slotArc/2, slotCenter + slotArc/2, 15);
        fill([0.2*cos(slotTheta) 0.5*cos(fliplr(slotTheta))], ...
            [0.2*sin(slotTheta) 0.5*sin(fliplr(slotTheta))], ...
            coilColors(mod(coilIndex - 1, size(coilColors, 1)) + 1, :), FaceAlpha=0.55, EdgeColor="k")
        text(0.36*cos(slotCenter), 0.36*sin(slotCenter), string(coilIndex), ...
            HorizontalAlignment="center", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=8, Color="k")
    end
end

text(0, -1.25, string(c) + " coils, " + string(p) + " pole pairs, " + string(pulseCount) + " pulses/rev", ...
    HorizontalAlignment="center", FontSize=8)
hold off
axis equal
axis off
% Add legend: one entry per coil (turn) color and entries for N/S pole colors
% Build coil labels and colors (matches colororder used in drawMotorCrossSection)
coilColors = colororder;
numLegendCoils = min(c, size(coilColors,1));
coilLabels = arrayfun(@(k) "Coil " + string(k), 1:numLegendCoils, "UniformOutput", false);

% Create invisible patch objects for coil colors to use in legend
hCoils = gobjects(1,numLegendCoils);
for k = 1:numLegendCoils
    hCoils(k) = patch(nan, nan, coilColors(mod(k-1, size(coilColors,1))+1, :), ...
        'FaceAlpha', 0.55, 'EdgeColor', 'none', 'Visible','on');
end

% Create sample patches for N and S pole colors (must match colors in drawMotorCrossSection)
hN = patch(nan, nan, [0.85 0.3 0.3], 'FaceAlpha', 0.55, 'EdgeColor', 'none', 'Visible','on');
hS = patch(nan, nan, [0.3 0.3 0.85], 'FaceAlpha', 0.55, 'EdgeColor', 'none', 'Visible','on');

% Assemble legend entries and labels
legendHandles = [hCoils, hN, hS];
legendLabels = [coilLabels, "Pole: N", "Pole: S"];

% Place legend outside the plot area at bottom-right of the cross-section tile
legend(legendHandles, legendLabels, Location="eastoutside", FontSize=8, Box='off')
end
%%
%[text] ### Design Challenge
%[text] ![Exercise icon](text:image:941c) **Exercise 5.** Click Generate Challenge to receive a random motor design challenge. This is a constrained design problem: you are balancing torque, smoothness, and speed rather than maximizing one number. If a constraint fails, change one parameter at a time and predict the trade-off before checking again. Here is a quick reference:
%[text] - **N** (turns), **B** (field), **Coil Radius**, and **NumCoils** affect torque and speed through $K\_{\\text{eff}} = (2/\\pi)cNAB$
%[text] - **NumCoils** also affects torque ripple; more coils make smoother torque
%[text] - **PolePairs** multiplies torque pulses per revolution but does not change the ideal speed-torque line here
%[text] - **V** (voltage) and **R** (resistance) affect speed $\\omega\_0 = V/K\_e$ and stall torque $\\tau\_{\\text{stall}} = K\_tV/R$ \
  %[control:button:ma_gen5]{"position":[1,2]}
Prob5 = GenerateDesignChallenge();

function Prob = GenerateDesignChallenge()
challenges = {
    struct(MinTorque=0.005, MaxRipple=50, MinSpeed=500, Name="Drone propeller motor")
    struct(MinTorque=0.08,  MaxRipple=10, MinSpeed=50,  Name="Robotic arm actuator")
    struct(MinTorque=0.03,  MaxRipple=5,  MinSpeed=300, Name="Power drill motor")
    };
idx = randi(length(challenges));
Prob = challenges{idx};
Prob.ID = "Ex5";
showDesignChallenge(Prob)
end

function showDesignChallenge(Prob)
labels = ["Average torque", "Torque ripple", "No-load speed"];
values = [
    ">= " + string(round(Prob.MinTorque, 3)) + " N·m"
    "<= " + string(round(Prob.MaxRipple)) + "%"
    ">= " + string(round(Prob.MinSpeed)) + " rad/s"
    ];

figure(Position=[100 100 650 270])
axis off
xlim([0 1])
ylim([0 1])
rectangle(Position=[0.02 0.06 0.96 0.88], Curvature=[0.04 0.04], ...
    FaceColor=[0.96 0.98 1.0], EdgeColor=[0.45 0.55 0.65], LineWidth=1.2)
text(0.06, 0.82, "Design Challenge", FontWeight="bold", FontSize=14)
text(0.06, 0.70, Prob.Name, FontWeight="bold", FontSize=18, Color=[0.10 0.22 0.34])

cardLeft = [0.06 0.37 0.68];
cardWidth = 0.26;
for cardIndex = 1:3
    rectangle(Position=[cardLeft(cardIndex) 0.34 cardWidth 0.24], Curvature=[0.08 0.08], ...
        FaceColor=[1 1 1], EdgeColor=[0.72 0.76 0.80])
    text(cardLeft(cardIndex) + 0.02, 0.51, labels(cardIndex), FontSize=8, Color=[0.35 0.40 0.45])
    text(cardLeft(cardIndex) + 0.02, 0.42, values(cardIndex), FontSize=13, FontWeight="bold")
end

text(0.06, 0.18, "Adjust the design parameters, then click Check Design.", ...
    FontSize=10, Color=[0.25 0.30 0.35])
end
%%
%[text] %[text:anchor:EX5_CHECK] Adjust these controls, then click **Check Design** to validate your motor design against the challenge specifications.
DesignTurns = 10; % Number of turns %[control:spinner:7554]{"position":[15,17]}
DesignField = 0.5; % Magnetic field (T) %[control:editfield:9549]{"position":[15,18]}
DesignCoilRadius = 0.05; % Coil radius (m) %[control:editfield:287c]{"position":[20,24]}
NumCoils = 2; % Number of coils %[control:spinner:5155]{"position":[12,13]}
PolePairs = 1; % Number of pole pairs %[control:spinner:2e64]{"position":[13,14]}
DesignVoltage = 12; % Supply voltage (V) %[control:editfield:3dad]{"position":[17,19]}
DesignResistance = 1; % Winding resistance (Ω) %[control:editfield:3bb4]{"position":[20,21]}

  %[control:button:ma_chk5]{"position":[1,2]}
if exist("Prob5", "var") && isfield(Prob5, "ID") && Prob5.ID == "Ex5"
    Design = struct( ...
        "NumCoils", NumCoils, ...
        "Turns", DesignTurns, ...
        "CoilRadius", DesignCoilRadius, ...
        "Field", DesignField, ...
        "PolePairs", PolePairs, ...
        "Voltage", DesignVoltage, ...
        "Resistance", DesignResistance);
    CheckExercise5(Prob5, Design);
else
    warning("Click Generate Challenge first.")
end

function CheckExercise5(Prob, Design)
if isDefaultDesign(Design)
    displayFormula('"' + "Adjust the design values before checking your design." + '"')
    return
end

if ~isValidDesign(Design)
    warning("Use practical positive values: 1-12 coils, 1-500 turns, radius 0.005-0.20 m, B 0.01-2 T, 1-8 pole pairs, V > 0, and R > 0.")
    return
end

A = pi * Design.CoilRadius^2;
c = max(1, round(Design.NumCoils));
p = max(1, round(Design.PolePairs));

Ke = computeEffectiveMotorConstant(Design.Turns, A, Design.Field, c);
Kt = Ke;
omega0 = Design.Voltage / Ke;
tauStall = Kt * Design.Voltage / Design.Resistance;

% Recompute the same design metrics used by the dashboard.
theta = linspace(0, 2*pi, 500);
thetaElec = p * theta;
offsets = (0:c-1) * pi / c;
shape = zeros(size(theta));
for k = 1:c
    shape = shape + abs(sin(thetaElec + offsets(k)));
end
shape = shape * pi / (2*c);
ripple = (max(shape) - min(shape)) / (max(shape) + min(shape)) * 100;
tauAvg = tauStall * mean(shape);

torqueOK = tauAvg >= Prob.MinTorque;
rippleOK = ripple <= Prob.MaxRipple;
speedOK = omega0 >= Prob.MinSpeed;

if torqueOK && rippleOK && speedOK
    displayFormula('"' + "Congratulations! Your motor design meets all specifications!" + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "  Average torque: " + string(round(tauAvg, 4)) + " N·m (target: >= " + string(round(Prob.MinTorque, 3)) + ")" + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "  Torque ripple: " + string(round(ripple, 1)) + "% (target: <= " + string(round(Prob.MaxRipple)) + "%)" + '"')
    displayFormula('"' + "  No-load speed: " + string(round(omega0, 1)) + " rad/s (target: >= " + string(round(Prob.MinSpeed)) + ")" + '"')
else
    if ~torqueOK
        warning("Torque too low: " + string(round(tauAvg, 4)) + " N·m (need >= " + string(round(Prob.MinTorque, 3)) + "). Increasing N, B, or radius helps torque, but can reduce no-load speed.")
    end
    if ~rippleOK
        warning("Ripple too high: " + string(round(ripple, 1)) + "% (need <= " + string(round(Prob.MaxRipple)) + "%). Try adding more coils; pole pairs increase pulse frequency but do not reduce this ripple metric.")
    end
    if ~speedOK
        warning("Speed too low: " + string(round(omega0, 1)) + " rad/s (need >= " + string(round(Prob.MinSpeed)) + "). Try increasing V, or decreasing N, radius, or B.")
    end
end
end

function isDefault = isDefaultDesign(Design)
isDefault = Design.NumCoils == 4 && ...
    Design.Turns == 5 && ...
    abs(Design.CoilRadius - 0.05) < eps(0.05) && ...
    abs(Design.Field - 0.5) < eps(0.5) && ...
    Design.PolePairs == 3 && ...
    abs(Design.Voltage - 12) < eps(12) && ...
    abs(Design.Resistance - 1) < eps(1);
end

function isValid = isValidDesign(Design)
isValid = Design.NumCoils >= 1 && Design.NumCoils <= 12 && ...
    Design.Turns >= 1 && Design.Turns <= 500 && ...
    Design.CoilRadius >= 0.005 && Design.CoilRadius <= 0.20 && ...
    Design.Field >= 0.01 && Design.Field <= 2 && ...
    Design.PolePairs >= 1 && Design.PolePairs <= 8 && ...
    Design.Voltage > 0 && Design.Resistance > 0;
end

function K = computeEffectiveMotorConstant(N, A, B, NumCoils)
if nargin < 4
    NumCoils = 1;
end

K = (2/pi) * max(1, round(NumCoils)) * N * A * B;
end

function limits = computePaddedLimits(values)
finiteValues = values(isfinite(values));
if isempty(finiteValues)
    limits = [-1 1];
    return
end

lowValue = min(finiteValues);
highValue = max(finiteValues);
if lowValue == highValue
    padding = max(1, abs(lowValue) * 0.1);
else
    padding = 0.1 * (highValue - lowValue);
end
limits = [lowValue - padding, highValue + padding];
end
%%
%[text] ## Further Exploration
%[text] The brushed DC motor you studied here is one member of a large family. All electric motors use the same basic idea, current and magnetic fields creating forces, but they differ in how they manage commutation, create the magnetic field, and control the current. The table below gives you a map for what to study next.
%[text:table]
%[text] | Motor Type | Key Difference from Brushed DC | Typical Applications |
%[text] | --- | --- | --- |
%[text] | Brushed DC | Mechanical commutation via brushes | Toys, small appliances, car windows |
%[text] | [Brushless DC (BLDC)](https://www.mathworks.com/videos/brushless-dc-motors-introduction-1564728874059.html) | Electronic commutation, no brushes | Drones, computer fans, electric vehicles |
%[text] | Permanent Magnet Synchronous (PMSM) | Sinusoidal drive, smoother than BLDC | Industrial servo drives, washing machines |
%[text] | Stepper | Discrete step rotation, open-loop position control | 3D printers, CNC machines, camera focus |
%[text] | Induction | No permanent magnets, rotor current induced by stator | Industrial pumps, HVAC, conveyor belts |
%[text] | Switched Reluctance | Simplest rotor (no magnets or windings) | Vacuum cleaners, aerospace actuators |
%[text] | Universal | Runs on AC or DC, high power-to-weight | Power drills, blenders, vacuum cleaners |
%[text:table]
